      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Wrote my own quick script, as follows:\n\n```\n\nusing System.Collections;\n\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\n\nusing UnityEngine;\n\npublic class counter : MonoBehaviour\n\n{\n\n    public GameObject target;\n\n    // Start is called before the first frame update\n\n    void Start()\n\n    {\n\n        if(target==null){\n\n            return;\n\n        }\n\n        static int CountPolys(Mesh mesh){\n\n            //seems to be the \"correct\" number regardless of whether mesh.GetTopology(0) == MeshTopology.Triangles or MeshTopology.Quads\n\n            return mesh.triangles.Length/3;\n\n        }\n\n        SkinnedMeshRenderer[] skinnedMeshes=target.GetComponentsInChildren<SkinnedMeshRenderer>();\n\n        int skinnedPolyCount = 0;\n\n        foreach(SkinnedMeshRenderer meshFilter in skinnedMeshes)\n\n        {\n\n            skinnedPolyCount += CountPolys(meshFilter.sharedMesh);\n\n        }\n\n        MeshFilter[] meshes=target.GetComponentsInChildren<MeshFilter>();\n\n        int polyCount = 0;\n\n        foreach(MeshFilter meshFilter in meshes)\n\n        {\n\n            polyCount += CountPolys(meshFilter.sharedMesh);\n\n        }\n\n        Debug.Log(\"Found \"+polyCount+\" polys in \"+meshes.Length+\" meshes\");\n\n        Debug.Log(\"Found \"+skinnedPolyCount+\" polys in \"+skinnedMeshes.Length+\" skinned meshes\");\n\n        Debug.Log(\"Found \"+(polyCount+skinnedPolyCount)+\" polys in \"+(meshes.Length + skinnedMeshes.Length)+\" total meshes\");\n\n    }\n\n    // Update is called once per frame\n\n    void Update()\n\n    {\n\n        \n\n    }\n\n}\n\n```\n\nI notice that while the count seems to be 100% correct for static meshes, it's slightly off(?) for skinned meshes.  That said, simply doing the above produces a count of 74172 triangles (regardless of whether or not keep quads is used) for my 37210 quad mesh, which I'd expect to have 74420 triangles.  This is far better than the sdk's current, and quite frankly absurd assertion of 124030 triangles.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
